.TH SNUFFLES 1 "August 2026" "snuffles 1.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
snuffles \- lightweight terminal network packet analyzer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snuffles
[\fB\-i\fR \fIiface\fR] [\fB\-r\fR \fIfile.pcap\fR] [\fB\-f\fR \fIfilter\fR]
[\fB\-c\fR \fIcount\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fIsnaplen\fR] [\fB\-b\fR \fIring\fR]
[\fB\-\-arena\-mb\fR \fIMB\fR]
[\fB\-B\fR \fIMB\fR] [\fB\-\-immediate\fR]
[\fB\-o\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-w\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-ui\fR] [\fB\-\-jsonl\fR]
[\fB\-q\fR] [\fB\-\-syslog\fR \fIhost:port\fR] [\fB\-\-stats\fR[=\fIfile\fR]]
[\fB\-\-no\-summary\fR] [\fB\-\-cpu\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-rt\fR] [\fB\-\-list\-ifaces\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snuffles
captures and dissects network traffic with an interactive terminal UI:
Wireshark\-style display filters, TCP session tracking, protocol statistics,
streaming pcap output, JSON Lines output, syslog forwarding, and PCAP/JSON
export. It dissects Ethernet, VLAN, ARP, Linux cooked capture, loopback
framing, IPv4/IPv6 (extension headers included), ICMP, TCP, UDP, SCTP,
DNS, HTTP/1.x and TLS (SNI).
.PP
Live capture requires elevated privileges; snuffles drops root after the
capture socket is open.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-i " iface"
Capture interface (default: auto\-detect).
.TP
.BI \-r " file.pcap"
Read packets from a pcap file (libpcap build only). Into a headless
consumer (\fB\-\-no\-ui\fR, \fB\-\-jsonl\fR) the reader waits for the
consumer instead of overwriting records it has not emitted yet, so every
packet of the file is output whatever the ring size.
.TP
.BI \-f " filter"
BPF capture filter. libpcap build: full pcap syntax. Raw\-socket build
(Linux): subset \(em tcp/udp/icmp/sctp/ip/arp, [src|dst] host \fIA.B.C.D\fR,
[src|dst] port \fIN\fR, joined with \fBand\fR.
.TP
.BI \-c " count"
Stop after \fIcount\fR packets.
.TP
.BI \-s " snaplen"
Snapshot length in bytes (64\-65535). Default 65535 in the TUI; 1518 (one
Ethernet frame with a VLAN tag) in the headless modes
.RB ( \-\-no\-ui ,
.BR \-\-jsonl ,
.BR \-q )
and with
.BR \-w ,
unless set here or in the config file — nothing there shows more than
the headers, and a kernel that coalesces packets (GRO/GSO) would
otherwise hand over 64 KB super\-frames. Raw\-socket build
(Linux): with the TPACKET_V3 ring it is applied in the kernel through the
filter program's return value, so at most \fIsnaplen\fR bytes of each
frame are copied into the ring, with or without
.BR \-f ;
the
.BR recvmmsg (2)
path truncates in user space so the wire length stays known.
.TP
.BI \-b " ring"
Ring buffer size in packets (16\-1000000, default 10000). Each record
costs about 350 bytes; the packet bytes live in the payload arena
.RB ( \-\-arena\-mb ).
.TP
.BI \-\-arena\-mb " MB"
Size of the payload arena shared by all ring records (1\-65536; default
\fIring\fR x min(\fIsnaplen\fR, 2048) bytes, i.e. 20 MB for the default
ring). Packets take only the bytes they need, bump\-allocated in a
circle: when the arena wraps before the ring does (frames larger than
2 KB on average), the oldest records keep their summary but lose their
bytes \(em the TUI hex view says so and
.B \-o
writes them with a captured length of 0. A packet longer than the arena
is cut to the arena size.
.TP
.BI \-B " MB"
Kernel capture buffer size in megabytes (1\-2047, default 64). This is the
buffer the kernel fills while the capture thread is not scheduled, so it
decides how much burst or scheduling jitter can be absorbed before the
kernel drops packets; the kernel may cap or shrink the request. Every
capture worker gets one of this size, so
.B \-j
.I N
reserves N times as much.
libpcap build: the TPACKET ring mapped into the process.
Raw\-socket build (Linux): a TPACKET_V3 ring of 256\ KiB blocks mapped into
the process; the kernel hands over a block when it is full or 10\ ms after
its first frame, with kernel timestamps. If it cannot provide the ring
(ENOMEM, a pre\-3.2 kernel) a note is printed and the packet socket's
receive queue is used instead, set with SO_RCVBUFFORCE before privileges
are dropped (falls back to SO_RCVBUF, capped by
.BR net.core.rmem_max ,
with a warning; the kernel itself caps a socket buffer at 1024\ MiB), frames
read in batches of 64 with
.BR recvmmsg (2).
.TP
.B \-\-immediate
Deliver every packet as soon as it arrives instead of in 10\ ms batches.
Lower per\-packet latency at a higher per\-packet CPU cost; on Linux this
switches libpcap from TPACKET_V3 blocks to a per\-frame ring, and the
raw\-socket build from its TPACKET_V3 ring to
.BR recvmmsg (2)
reads.
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Export the ring buffer on exit; format chosen by extension
(\fB.pcap\fR or \fB.json\fR).
.TP
.BI \-w " file"
Stream every captured packet to a pcap file while capturing.
.B \-w \-
writes to stdout (combine with \fB\-q\fR), e.g. for
.BR "snuffles \-q \-w \- | wireshark \-k \-i \-" .
Written by the output thread from the ring buffer through a 1\ MB buffer
flushed whenever it goes idle; a disk that cannot keep up costs records
(\fIout_missed\fR in \fB\-\-stats\fR), never capture.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ui
Headless mode: print one line per packet to stdout.
.TP
.B \-\-jsonl
Headless mode emitting one JSON object per packet (JSON Lines).
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
No packet output (only the exit summary on stderr, see
\fB\-\-no\-summary\fR); use with \fB\-\-syslog\fR or \fB\-w\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-syslog " host:port"
Forward per\-packet CSV records over UDP syslog. An output thread reads
the records from the ring buffer (the capture thread is never slowed by
the collector), batches them (up to 32 per non\-blocking
.BR sendmmsg (2)
on Linux) and flushes whenever it catches up; the socket gets a 16\ MB
send buffer. Datagrams the kernel cannot queue are dropped, never waited
for, and counted as
.I syslog_fail
by
.BR \-\-stats ;
records the ring overwrote before the thread reached them are counted as
.IR out_missed .
Without \fB\-b\fR the quiet/headless syslog forwarder sizes the ring to the
kernel capture buffer (8192 slots per MB of \fB\-B\fR, 4096\-65536), so a
burst the capture thread hands over in one go cannot lap the output threads.
.TP
.BI \-\-syslog\-iface " ip|dev"
Source address or device for syslog datagrams.
.TP
.BI \-\-syslog\-threads " N|auto"
The most syslog output threads (1\-16), each with its own UDP socket;
\fBauto\fR (the default) means one per CPU the process may run on, at most
16. One thread hands the kernel roughly 200\-330k datagrams/s, the cost
being the kernel's per\-datagram send path, so threads are created and
retired with the traffic: only one runs at start; workers claim records in
32\-record chunks from a shared cursor and keep a running busy fraction;
when the unclaimed backlog exceeds an eighth of the ring, or the running
threads average 85\ % busy with records queued, a running thread wakes a
parked one or creates a new one (at most one every 2\ ms); a helper parks
when the others would average no more than 70\ % busy without it, and a
parked thread exits after 3\ s without work. The record format is
unchanged; the collector sees up to \fIN\fR source ports. With \fB\-w\fR the
stream runs on a thread of its own.
.TP
.BI \-\-syslog\-min\-threads " N"
Syslog output threads that exist always (1\-16, default 1). Equal to
\fB\-\-syslog\-threads\fR it pins the count.
.TP
.BR \-\-stats [=\fIfile\fR]
Print a counters line (captured, kernel and interface drops \(em per
worker as well with \fB\-j\fR, ring
occupancy, records emitted and missed by the consumer, syslog and stream
counts, records missed by the syslog and by the stream output threads,
the most syslog threads alive at once and alive now, sessions, wake\-ups,
RSS)
every second and a
.B summary
line at exit, to stderr or to \fIfile\fR (the TUI requires \fIfile\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-summary
Headless modes print the
.B summary
line at exit even without \fB\-\-stats\fR, followed by a hint when the
kernel dropped packets; this flag turns both off.
.B SIGUSR1
prints one
.B stats
line at any time (headless modes; with \fB\-\-stats=\fIfile\fR also in
the TUI). A frame longer than 1518 bytes on a non\-jumbo interface warns
once about GRO/GSO super\-frames (headless modes).
.TP
.BI \-j " N" "\fR, \fP" \-\-workers " N"
Capture with \fIN\fR workers (1\-16, default 1). The workers open \fIN\fR
sockets on the same interface in one PACKET_FANOUT group in
PACKET_FANOUT_HASH mode (group id: the process id), each with its own
thread, its own kernel buffer and its own session table shard; the kernel
hashes every frame's flow and always delivers it to the same socket, so a
flow stays on one worker and in order, and one interface's capture work
spreads over \fIN\fR cores. All workers feed the one ring buffer, share
the \fB\-c\fR budget and the \fB\-w\fR/\fB\-\-syslog\fR sinks, and their
session shards are shown as one table.
.B \-B
applies per worker, so total kernel memory is \fIN\fR x \fB\-B\fR.
Records reach the ring in the order the workers commit them, so with
.I N
> 1 the packet list,
.B \-w
and the exports are no longer strictly timestamp\-sorted across flows
(one flow's packets stay in order), and
.B \-\-cpu
would pin every worker to the same CPU.
Linux live capture only: reading a file with
.BR \-r ,
another platform, or a capture handle that is not an AF_PACKET socket
prints one warning and uses a single worker. With
.I N
> 1 the TUI title bar shows "\fIN\fR workers" and
.B \-\-stats
adds per\-worker kernel drops as \fBkdrop_w=\fIa\fB,\fIb\fB,\fIc\fR.
Config key
.BR workers .
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu " N"
Pin the capture thread to CPU \fIN\fR (Linux). The other threads (the
headless consumer or the TUI, the output and stats threads) are kept on the
remaining CPUs of the process's affinity mask. Config key
.BR cpu .
.TP
.B \-\-rt
Run the capture thread under SCHED_FIFO at priority 1 so a busy consumer
or display never deschedules it during a burst. Applied before privileges
are dropped, so it needs root or CAP_SYS_NICE; otherwise a warning is
printed and capture continues at normal priority. Combine with
\fB\-\-cpu\fR on machines with spare cores. Config key
.BR rt .
.TP
.B \-\-list\-ifaces
List capture interfaces and exit.
.TP
.B \-v
Print version. \fB\-h\fR prints usage.
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
Up/Down/PgUp/PgDn/Home/End scroll; Enter opens the packet detail panel
(Left/Right scroll its hex dump) or drills into a session;
.B S
toggles the sessions view,
.B T
cycles its sort;
.B V
shows protocol statistics;
.B /
searches (\fBn\fR/\fBN\fR next/previous);
.B F
sets the display filter,
.B B
the kernel BPF filter;
.B E
exports,
.B C
clears,
.B P
pauses,
.B Q
quits,
.B H
shows help.
.PP
The screen is redrawn at most 30 times a second (at once on a key press,
every 250\ ms when nothing changes), whatever the packet rate. The sessions
view works from a copy of the session table refreshed every 250\ ms, or
immediately on a view switch, sort change or page jump.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo snuffles \-i eth0
sudo snuffles \-i eth0 \-f "tcp port 443" \-w capture.pcap
sudo snuffles \-i eth0 \-\-jsonl | jq \-r .dst_ip
sudo snuffles \-i eth0 \-\-jsonl \-\-cpu 3 \-\-rt > packets.jsonl
kill \-USR1 $(pidof snuffles)
snuffles \-r traffic.pcap
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.snufflesrc
Optional configuration, read at startup and overridden by command\-line
flags. The path can be changed with the
.B SNUFFLES_CONFIG
environment variable; a missing file is silently ignored.
Format: \fB#\fR comments, blank lines, and \fIkey\fR \fB=\fR \fIvalue\fR
pairs with the keys
.BR interface ,
.BR snaplen " (64\-65535),"
.BR ring_size " (16\-1000000),"
.BR buffer_mb " (1\-2047),"
.BR arena_mb " (1\-65536),"
.BR promisc " (0/1),"
.BR syslog ,
.BR syslog_iface ,
.BR syslog_threads ,
.BR syslog_min_threads ,
.BR cpu " (0\-8191), and"
.BR rt " (0/1)."
Saved display\-filter presets are declared as
.B preset
.I name
.B =
.I expression
(name: alphanumeric/underscore/hyphen, at most 31 characters) and applied
from the TUI display\-filter prompt as \fB@\fIname\fR (case\-insensitive).
Invalid lines print a warning and are skipped; the config is never fatal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 on invalid arguments, capture error, or failed export.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tcpdump (8),
.BR wireshark (1),
.BR pcap\-filter (7)
